    "note": "Replaces a file with zero _provenance ('NIST SP 800-185 KMAC test vectors (Samples #1 and #4)' claimed as the source but no URL/hash ever recorded - unverifiable, treated as Tier 4 under this plan's ladder). Selection was constrained by a real engine limitation found before picking any vector: src/lib/crypto/OSSLKMAC.cpp's signInit/verifyInit call EVP_MAC_init with exactly one OSSL_PARAM (OSSL_MAC_PARAM_SIZE, fixed at construction to 32 bytes for OSSLKMAC128 / 64 bytes for OSSLKMAC256, see OSSLKMAC.h:40,46) and never set OSSL_MAC_PARAM_CUSTOM or OSSL_MAC_PARAM_XOF - there is no CK_KMAC_PARAMS parsing anywhere in src/lib/ (grepped) despite a comment at SoftHSM_sign.cpp:131 referencing one, so the C++ engine can ONLY produce non-XOF KMAC with an EMPTY customization string and output length fixed at 256 bits (KMAC-128) / 512 bits (KMAC-256), regardless of what a caller requests. Confirmed empirically: `openssl mac -macopt hexkey:<K> -macopt size:32 KMAC-128 -in <msg>` (same EVP_MAC call shape, no custom/xof macopt) reproduces NIST SP 800-185's Sample #4 exactly (E5780B0D...F456EE14E). Rust's KMAC path (ffi.rs:5852-5863 verify / :5563-5575 sign dispatch to crypto::handlers::sign_kmac_ext, handlers.rs:1452) is NOT similarly constrained - it reads a caller-supplied output length and customization string directly from the raw mechanism-parameter bytes (u32 output length + trailing customization, ffi.rs:5564-5571 / :5852-5860) - so this file's constraint is C++-driven. Searched both the KMAC-128-1.0 and KMAC-256-1.0 internalProjection.json (this pinned commit, isSample:false - a real, fully-randomized generation, not a curated demo set) for a non-XOF, empty-customization, byte-aligned-length case at exactly macLen=256 (KMAC-128) or 512 (KMAC-256): across all 400 non-XOF test cases in KMAC-128-1.0, exactly ONE matches all four constraints - tgId 8 (MVT), tcId 771, used here. KMAC-256-1.0 has ZERO matches at macLen=512 under the same constraints (3 empty-customization/byte-aligned cases exist in that file, at macLen 3222/2550/1351 bits - none usable against this engine's fixed 512-bit output) - so this file carries KMAC-128 evidence only; KMAC-256 has no engine-compatible real ACVP vector at this pinned commit. The one usable case is a NEGATIVE (MVT, testPassed:false) test: NIST's own tool paired this key/message with a deliberately-wrong mac to test rejection, not an AFT-style 'compute the correct answer' case - a real AFT match satisfying all four constraints does not exist in this pinned sample. Verified independently before writing: pycryptodome's KMAC128.new(key=..., mac_len=32, custom=b'') over the real message confirms the CORRECT mac is 6ea1fb522bffa034c35def86c1bd44bae335a3293e9b88af97814a01f78620df, NOT the file's stated (deliberately wrong) 71cb6f914872a4967e736aca16389386ce147e4801e464e0415cad3aa5f3cb88 - confirming this is genuinely the NIST-authored negative case and not corrupted in transcription. Not wired into tests/acvp-wasm.mjs or tests/helpers.mjs: no KMAC sign/verify helper exists there at all (unlike PBKDF2, which already had one), and building one correctly needs to match Rust's raw mechanism-parameter layout for CKM_KMAC_128/256 (first 4 bytes little-endian u32 output length, remaining bytes literal customization string - ffi.rs:5564-5571) as well as C++'s param-free path (SoftHSM_sign.cpp/OSSLKMAC.cpp take no parameter at all) - left for a follow-up change; this file is now correct and real, but not yet exercised by any test."
